  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nThe first half of next week will continue to bring rain chances\nacross the eastern half of Arizona as the Pacific trough lingers\njust to our north and northwest and the ridge stays centered over\nthe Southeastern U.S. This will continue place Arizona in a south\nsouthwesterly flow area providing periods of moisture advection \nout of moisture rich northern/western Mexico. The biggest \nquestion that remains is what day or days will represent the best \nchances for more than just scattered showers and isolated storms.\nModels do agree plentiful moisture will remain over eastern and \ncentral Arizona through at least the middle of next week with \nPWATs running 150-180% of normal and low level mixing ratios \nanywhere from 11-14 g/kg. However, there are uncertainties with \nthe amount of instability due to a good deal of forecast cloud \ncover and lack of any obvious trigger mechanisms. Last night's \nmodel runs were more optimistic with upper level support and this \nwill likely be a piece of the puzzle needed for an active weather \nday given the questionable instability and inhibition. \n\nThe NBM is still honing in on late Monday through Tuesday as the\nbest potential for showers and thunderstorms across eastern and\ncentral Arizona, but confidence in this is still fairly low. It\nwould not be surprising to have any day from Monday through \nThursday to be a good storm day, but it is still too early to \npinpoint which day or days. With the upper level support leaning \ntoward being less of an influence, we may just have to rely on \ndifferential heating and/or wherever the western edge of the \nmoisture axis sets up. Forecast instability does look to be enough\nto support some strong storms and with the moisture levels \nexpected, heavy rainfall leading to localized flooding could also\nbecome an issue. Overall, rain chances for next week look quite \nconditional and PoPs are likely to change. Forecast temperatures \nfor next week have also been trending upward over the past few \ndays with decreasing chances of having lower desert highs dipping \nbelow 100 degrees on any day. The latest NBM shows highs hovering\nright around normal to a few degrees above.",
